WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:06.440
Now, if you is not like a query that you can use in SQL, it is an object that we can find in the database.

00:06.520 --> 00:11.960
So before we jump immediately to the view, I would like to give you the big picture, the whole structure

00:11.960 --> 00:13.040
of the database.

00:13.120 --> 00:13.880
So let's go.

00:13.920 --> 00:15.880
We have like hierarchy structure.

00:16.080 --> 00:20.000
And the highest level of this hierarchy is the SQL server.

00:20.040 --> 00:23.000
The SQL server manages multiple databases.

00:23.000 --> 00:27.200
It's like the control center that keeps everything running and accessible.

00:27.240 --> 00:30.560
Now inside the SQL server we have multiple databases.

00:30.560 --> 00:35.560
So a database is a collection of affirmations that are stored in structured way.

00:35.600 --> 00:40.400
It's where all your data is kept and organized in different tables and objects.

00:40.400 --> 00:44.440
And each database is separated from others and it has its own data.

00:44.480 --> 00:48.520
Now inside each database we can find multiple schemas.

00:48.560 --> 00:55.200
A schema is like a logical way on how you group up related objects like tables and views together within

00:55.240 --> 00:56.080
a database.

00:56.120 --> 01:01.240
Like for example, if you have a database called sales, we can group up different tables about the

01:01.240 --> 01:03.600
orders underneath the schema orders.

01:03.640 --> 01:08.920
And maybe we have like multiple views and tables about the customers where we can put it in the schema

01:08.920 --> 01:09.600
customers.

01:09.600 --> 01:15.320
So if you find like multiple tables and views that are describing the same object, the same topic,

01:15.320 --> 01:18.360
we put them all together underneath one schema.

01:18.360 --> 01:23.120
So again, a database could be like the sales database and the HR database.

01:23.120 --> 01:25.440
They are completely different types of data.

01:25.440 --> 01:28.600
And underneath the sales we can have like different sections.

01:28.600 --> 01:32.200
We have sections about the orders and sections about the customers.

01:32.240 --> 01:36.240
And now moving on what we can find inside the schema we can find tables.

01:36.240 --> 01:39.160
A table is where actually your data is stored.

01:39.160 --> 01:41.640
It contains multiple columns and rows.

01:41.640 --> 01:43.880
So it is where the data physically lives.

01:43.920 --> 01:48.360
And now inside the schemas we have another type of object we call it view.

01:48.360 --> 01:51.120
And of course in this section we are focusing on the views.

01:51.120 --> 01:55.360
So a view is like a virtual table that has a structure and everything.

01:55.360 --> 01:57.920
But inside it we don't have any data.

01:58.240 --> 02:00.640
So the view does not store any data.

02:00.640 --> 02:05.000
And in order to see the data, we have to execute the query behind the view.

02:05.040 --> 02:07.240
And only after that we are going to see some data.

02:07.240 --> 02:08.840
But it is not like the tables.

02:08.840 --> 02:10.990
It doesn't store the data permanently.

02:10.990 --> 02:15.550
Now, inside the tables we can define multiple stuff like columns and as well keys.

02:15.590 --> 02:19.990
And the same thing for the views inside the views we can define multiple columns.

02:19.990 --> 02:24.270
And one last level for each column we have like a name and a data type.

02:24.310 --> 02:26.830
So as you can see the databases are really organized.

02:26.830 --> 02:33.710
And we have like hierarchy where the top node is the SQL server and the lowest node is the columns and

02:33.710 --> 02:34.310
rows.

02:34.310 --> 02:37.230
So this is what we call the database structure.

02:37.270 --> 02:42.070
Now in order for you to build and manage this structure we have set of commands.

02:42.070 --> 02:46.750
We call it DDL the shortcut of data definition language.

02:46.750 --> 02:53.830
So the detail is a set of commands that allow us to define and manage the structure of the database.

02:53.830 --> 03:00.190
So we have commands like create where it helps us to create databases, schemas, tables, views.

03:00.230 --> 03:01.750
Another command called alter.

03:01.790 --> 03:06.390
Of course after you create something you would like, maybe later to do changes and updates.

03:06.390 --> 03:11.910
And of course we have the drop in order to remove any database object like dropping a schema, dropping

03:11.910 --> 03:13.910
a database, tables, views.

03:13.910 --> 03:18.150
So as you can see, the DDL commands can help us to manage the database structure.

03:18.150 --> 03:24.070
So from this picture we have understood that we can create views inside schemas in the database.

03:24.070 --> 03:28.950
So now if you check the client at the Object explorer you can find the exact hierarchy.

03:28.950 --> 03:30.950
So it starts with the SQL server.

03:30.990 --> 03:33.710
This is our local server that runs at our machine.

03:33.710 --> 03:36.710
And then we can find inside it multiple databases.

03:36.710 --> 03:42.270
And one of them is our sales DB that you have installed together with other database like the Adventureworks.

03:42.310 --> 03:46.710
So now if you go to the sales DB over here, you can go and drill to the next level.

03:46.710 --> 03:48.830
And now we can find here a lot of objects.

03:48.830 --> 03:51.790
And one of them that we know we have tables and views.

03:51.830 --> 03:52.830
And now you might say okay.

03:52.870 --> 03:56.230
But between the database and tables we have schemas.

03:56.470 --> 03:57.750
So where are the schemas.

03:57.830 --> 04:03.070
Well actually if you go inside the tables you can find our tables customers employees and so on.

04:03.070 --> 04:07.550
But before it we have a name called sales dot customers.

04:07.550 --> 04:11.590
And you can find it everywhere sales customers sales employees and so on.

04:11.630 --> 04:17.950
The sales is the schema that bring all those tables together underneath one logical schema.

04:17.950 --> 04:20.900
So we have a database called sales DB.

04:20.940 --> 04:24.980
We have a schema called sales and we have a table called customers.

04:25.020 --> 04:29.060
And now if we would like to see all the schemas inside this database, what you can do, you can go

04:29.060 --> 04:30.500
to the securities over here.

04:30.500 --> 04:32.820
And then here we have like a folder called schemas.

04:32.820 --> 04:37.660
If you go over there you will find the list of all schemas that we have in this database.

04:37.660 --> 04:38.180
You might say.

04:38.180 --> 04:39.860
But we didn't create all those stuff.

04:39.860 --> 04:41.900
We have only the sales that we know.

04:41.940 --> 04:48.420
Well as you create a database in SQL server, you will get a lot of other system default schemas that

04:48.420 --> 04:49.660
the server can create.

04:49.740 --> 04:55.740
One of them is the information schema, where it holds a lot of views about the catalog and the metadata,

04:55.740 --> 04:58.940
where you can find the list of columns, tables, views, and so on.

04:58.940 --> 05:01.820
So here we have only one schema that we have created for the user.

05:01.820 --> 05:02.980
It is the sales.

05:03.380 --> 05:04.820
So let's go back.

05:04.820 --> 05:10.100
Now if you go inside one of those tables you will find here multiple stuff like we have columns, keys,

05:10.100 --> 05:11.460
constraints and so on.

05:11.660 --> 05:16.500
And if you go to the columns you will end up at the lowest level of the hierarchy.

05:16.500 --> 05:21.780
And here we have the columns like the customer ID and we have some extra informations like the data

05:21.820 --> 05:23.140
type length and so on.

05:23.140 --> 05:26.300
So this is the structure and the hierarchy of databases.
